those proteins look like.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:10.740
And so to make that a little
bit more clear to you,

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:14.410
when I was in grad school, I
studied this bacterium here,

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:18.070
and this bacterium is called
Streptococcus pneumoniae.

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:20.090
And it causes the disease, pneumonia,

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:22.720
which is a disease of your lungs.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:24.700
And so when you look a little closer

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:27.520
into the strep pneumoniae cells,

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:30.490
bacterial cells, you see
all this murky stuff.

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:34.150
And in that mixture of murky stuff

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:36.540
is where the bulk of the proteins are

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:41.260
that this disease causing
bacterium needs to survive.

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:43.360
And when you take an even closer look

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:46.240
at this murky stuff inside these cells,

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.854
this is what you see.

00:05:48.854 --> 00:05:53.010
And, so all of these
different colored objects

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:56.010
are what is found inside a bacterial cell.

00:05:56.010 --> 00:06:01.000
And your cells probably
look very similar to this

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.710
sort of, if not a bit more crowded.

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:06.010
