00:59:30.000 --> 00:59:30.833
Okay.

00:59:34.700 --> 00:59:36.390
So what's happening.

00:59:36.390 --> 00:59:38.950
So when we added acid to the tube,

00:59:38.950 --> 00:59:42.620
we saw that as we added more acid,

00:59:42.620 --> 00:59:46.130
the protein stopped fluorescing.

00:59:46.130 --> 00:59:48.630
And then we added base to the tube

00:59:48.630 --> 00:59:52.063
but we saw that the
fluorescence was restored.

00:59:53.020 --> 00:59:55.800
And so what we're seeing
here is an example

00:59:55.800 --> 00:59:57.040
of a chemical reaction called

00:59:57.040 --> 00:59:59.540
a replacement chemical reaction.

00:59:59.540 --> 01:00:01.490
And so what a replacement
chemical reaction is,

01:00:01.490 --> 01:00:05.440
is you have A plus BC, for example,

01:00:05.440 --> 01:00:07.450
and then the C no longer is with the B

01:00:07.450 --> 01:00:10.113
but joins together with
the A and produces B.

01:00:10.990 --> 01:00:12.340
And so what happened is

01:00:13.700 --> 01:00:16.160
we have our green fluorescent protein.

01:00:16.160 --> 01:00:19.253
We added an acid, hydrochloric acid,

01:00:20.420 --> 01:00:23.680
and so what happened is
the hydrochloric acid,

01:00:23.680 --> 01:00:26.130
the H part of the hydrochloric acid

01:00:26.130 --> 01:00:30.100
attached itself to the GFP
which stopped the chromophore

01:00:30.100 --> 01:00:31.460
from performing its function,

01:00:31.460 --> 01:00:34.020
of fluorescing, of emitting light.

01:00:34.020 --> 01:00:36.240
And so that's what we saw here.

01:00:36.240 --> 01:00:37.360
We saw a replacement reaction.

01:00:37.360 --> 01:00:40.390
And so then what happened
with the base is the opposite.

01:00:40.390 --> 01:00:42.500
So the base essentially

01:00:44.350 --> 01:00:48.360
displaced the hydrochloric
acid from the protein

01:00:48.360 --> 01:00:51.590
restoring the ability of the
chromophore to fluorescent.

01:00:51.590 --> 01:00:53.380
So that's what's happening here.

01:00:53.380 --> 01:00:56.810
So what happens is in
the presence of the acid,

01:00:56.810 --> 01:00:59.400
the H, the hydrogen from
the hydrochloric acid

01:00:59.400 --> 01:01:02.010
attaches to the nitrogen group of this

01:01:03.210 --> 01:01:08.210
aromatic ring here on the chromophore.

01:01:08.780 --> 01:01:10.660
And then when we added
base, it neutralized it.

01:01:10.660 --> 01:01:15.300
So it removed the hydrogen
from this nitrogen,

01:01:15.300 --> 01:01:18.440
returned the chromophore
back to its neutral state,

01:01:18.440 --> 01:01:21.523
allowing the protein to fluoresce again.

01:01:23.070 --> 01:01:28.033
And so that is the end

01:01:29.680 --> 01:01:32.993
of chemical reactions today.

